WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.250
Protest around statues

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.400
have been one of many polarizing moments,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.960
for Americans, and for
the rest of the world.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.500
And I promise we'll get to that.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.353
But first, let's talk about breakups.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:21.650
For a lot of people out there

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.000
who've spent a significant
amount of time with someone

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.860
only to have it end badly,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.490
a common cathartic process,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.400
is removing any imagery
that reminds you of them.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.000
Whether it's a gift they
gave you, or a trinket,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.160
or actual photographs, and
today, it's never been easier.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.680
Companies like Adobe and Apple

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.963
make it part of their
advertising campaigns.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.080
We all engage in some level
of editing our own history.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.000
We remember the good parts,
maybe forget the bad parts,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.520
but when this happens on a societal scale,

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.000
things become much more complicated.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.150
Especially when some people feel as if

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.340
they're the ones being erased.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.060
And when it comes to, say destroying art,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.370
well, we've been doing that

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.400
since the beginning of recorded history.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.950
As long as we've been making art,

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.470
we've been destroying it.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.140
That's Professor Erin Thompson

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.480
of John Jay College of Criminal Justice.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.380
And I'm an expert in the
deliberate destruction of art,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.903
and art crime in general.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.750
As long as someone has thought someone

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.120
is great enough to make a statue of,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.890
someone else has come along and thought,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.330
nope, we're tearing that down,

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.470
that person isn't great at all.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.040
I think of the Egyptians sculpting statues

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.960
of themselves to last for
eternity, to put in their tombs.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:32.820
For many Egyptians,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.430
eternity didn't last forever.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.030
Especially when it came to their noses.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:43.030
The most common question
that Egyptologists are asked,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.620
why are the noses broken
on ancient Egyptian art?

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.160
Research done by this man,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.720
Edward Bleiberg, shows that
this damage wasn't done

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.670
by the long march of time.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.890
Rather, it was a very specific
practice, called iconoclasm.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.240
This is the act of
attacking established values

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.900
and institutions, through
destroying symbols.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.500
Attacking a statue is somewhere
deep in the human brain.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.220
Something like attacking
that person themselves.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.600
In Egyptian mythology,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.300
statues were thought of
as spiritual vessels.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.710
So removing a nose,

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.620
was a way to disable that
connection to the afterlife,

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.420
but Bleiberg also theorizes
about another culprit

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:21.800
for this destruction,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.060
early Christian monks.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.530
Every new society when it's creating,

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.603
destroys images of the past.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.380
We see same thing happening
in the French revolution,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.670
with the overthrow of the
aristocratic government of France,

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.610
there is also an overthrow of statues.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.580
This is a cyclical process,

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.740
and it's happened countless
times throughout history.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.470
Once the society rises,

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.720
it builds statues to represent itself.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.860
And then as a new society comes along,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.390
it removes or destroys those old statues,

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:00.480
and replaces them, with their own.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.160
An example of that cycle
on a shorter timeline,

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.870
are the Christopher Columbus statues

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:06.460
across the United States.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.460
Statues of Columbus are fascinating to me

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.410
because they were put up,

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.570
at a time when Italians
were not necessarily

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.380
considered white.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.260
When whiteness was something
that was Northern European.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:21.940
Statues of Columbus were funded
by Italian-American groups

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:25.430
as a part of an argument that
we are, Italian American.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.830
Don't, reject us, don't
discriminate against us.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.890
Now, statues of Columbus

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.830
are being protested by native Americans

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.990
who see Columbus as a symbol,

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:36.880
of the fact that they are excluded

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.430
from belonging to America.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.510
And while the facts
around Columbus as a man

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.340
remain problematic,

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.200
as a symbol,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.300
he simultaneously stands
for mass genocide to some,

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.173
and national pride to others.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.350
What these, heritage sites and places,

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:58.000
statues and historic buildings do,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.500
is they give us a tangible
sense of the intangible.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.630
This is Professor Laurajane Smith.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.880
She's spent her career
researching, studying

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.770
and writing about heritage
and its effects on society,

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:10.510
including a decade long study

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:13.010
in how we interact with heritage sites.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.740
Most people go to reinforce
what they already know,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:16.763
feel and believe.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.580
And this is one of the issues,
with the heritage debate,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.330
is that, we have this sort of sense that,

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.000
heritage, particularly national heritage

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.360
has to make us feel good, and
feel good about ourselves.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.690
And when we visit these sites

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.040
to reinforce our deeply held beliefs,

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.510
it can blind us to realities,
right under our noses.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.530
Smith pointed to a series of interviews

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:42.000
she conducted at Andrew
Jackson's Hermitage in Tennessee,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.050
where visitors had to
walk through an exhibit

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.370
on the former president's role in slavery,

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.570
before they could enter his
palatial plantation house.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.260
Visitors, engage in an array of responses

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.180
that attempted to,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:56.520
shield themselves from the,

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.780
from the negative emotions that,

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.180
cut into their sense
of comfort for visiting

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.483
this presidential house.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.381
Some people would say, when I asked them,

00:05:06.381 --> 00:05:08.620
what did you think of the exhibition?

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:09.810
Would say,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.640
what exhibition? I didn't
notice any exhibition.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.600
So intent where they to get to,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.050
the comforting presidential performance.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.020
Others would go, oh yes
I saw it, but I'm not in,

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.490
you know, it's not relevant.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:21.620
It's not relevant to this,

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.653
to this history, I've
come to see, Jackson.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.530
While it may be natural for many of us

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:30.270
to avoid engaging with
the more complicated parts

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:31.560
of our history.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.330
These public forms of art,

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.663
are having an impact on us as a society.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.040
Neuroscientists talk
about the halo effect.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.733
And I like to talk about
the pedestal effect.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:49.010
When we see someone up on a pedestal,

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:50.420
all these assumptions kick in that,

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:52.530
they must be someone who's
there to be respected,

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.540
to be praised, to be honored.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:58.490
So statues are very bad
at communicating nuance

00:05:58.490 --> 00:05:59.900
and shades of gray.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.620
They are, what Michael
Bilig has referred to

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.920
as a form of banal nationalism,

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.770
they are so ubiquitous,

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.760
that we tend to, forget
that they're there,

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.120
but nonetheless,

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.810
are constantly referencing

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.150
and, representing particular ideas and,

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:18.940
group identity and so on.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:20.450
Identity is complicated.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:23.460
It's much easier to think of America

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:27.370
as a set of almost like
flashes of images of, the flag

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.540
and the Statue of Liberty,

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:31.160
a portrait of Washington.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:34.560
And that is, a shorthand for what it means

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:35.870
to be an American.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.890
So if you change those images,

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:41.093
that changes identity.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.090
Images summarize complex things

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:46.150
and if we want to change complex things

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.693
sometimes we need to change
those summary images.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.130
When we're not fighting actual wars,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.480
we often fight wars by using images,

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.610
by attacking the idea of

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.410
what the other side is standing for.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:03.040
To say, I don't want a white
supremacist America anymore,

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:04.760
you attack statues

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.060
that represents a white
supremacist America.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.590
But watching these images
change in real time,

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.260
is much different than walking through

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.240
an ancient iconoclasm exhibit in a museum.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.530
While removing or reclaiming a statue,

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.670
can give some that same cathartic sense

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:21.890
as removing an ex from a photo,

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:24.970
for others, it feels
like a personal attack.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.480
Not only on their past, but their future.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.341
If you don't,

00:07:29.341 --> 00:07:31.410
pay attention to history,
it repeats itself.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.390
Through history people have been horrified

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:36.920
by the destruction of statues because,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.290
that's a sign of our fragility,

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:43.130
that not only our bodies,
but our whole legacy,

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:47.270
any memory of us could be
overthrown, disrespected,

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:48.640
disregarded.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.990
So to defend a statue of really anybody,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.690
is to defend this idea that you yourself

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.750
might be around for the future.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:59.760
Heritage and history
are constant processes

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.630
of, reassessing who we
are and who we want to be.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.270
The debate will always be ongoing.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:06.103
And that,

00:08:06.103 --> 00:08:08.640
that in itself is I
think a far more healthy

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:09.920
and constructive,

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.170
way of approaching both
heritage and history.

